# Water sampling rod, bottle selection valve and water pump.
#
# This board has an IOMCU, so the FMU outputs are SERVO9..SERVO13, which appear
# as AUX1..AUX5 on the carrier board:
#
#   SERVO9  / AUX1 / GPIO 50  rod motor, servo PWM
#   SERVO10 / AUX2 / GPIO 51  bottle selection relay, plain GPIO
#   SERVO11 / AUX3 / GPIO 52  pump forward, plain GPIO
#   SERVO12 / AUX4 / GPIO 53  pump reverse, plain GPIO
#   SERVO13 / AUX5 / GPIO 54  pump command, unused output
#
# The rod and the three outputs the ground station writes to are on servo
# channels that no board has a pin for, so they can never collide with a
# physical output.
#
# The valve and the two pump lines are driven as plain GPIO, so the matching
# outputs must be released from the PWM timer: either SERVOn_FUNCTION=-1 or the
# channel bit in SERVO_GPIO_MASK does that. Setting the function to 0 is not
# enough - the pin stays owned by the timer and the GPIO writes are silently
# ignored, which leaves the pump lines sitting at 0V and the valve stuck on
# bottle 1. A channel marked -1 (GPIO) is also refused by MAV_CMD_DO_SET_SERVO,
# which is why the trigger and the pump command are not on those outputs.
WS_ENABLE,1
WS_MOTOR_CHAN,9
WS_TRIG_CHAN,14
WS_BOTTLE_CHAN,15
WS_VOLUME_CHAN,16
WS_PUMP_ENABLE,1
WS_PUMP_FWD_PIN,52
WS_PUMP_REV_PIN,53
WS_PUMP_CMD_CH,13
WS_VALVE_ENABLE,1
WS_VALVE_PIN,51
SERVO_GPIO_MASK,3584
SERVO10_FUNCTION,-1
SERVO11_FUNCTION,-1
SERVO12_FUNCTION,-1
